  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy conditions will continue across the area\ntoday.\n\nAnother upper-low centered over eastern portions of Ontario \ntoday will shift southeastward today, with a sharp trough axis \nwith strong shortwave energy pivoting through the region by this\nafternoon. At the surface, low pressure will pass north of the \narea today with a trailing cold front moving through the \nregion. High pressure will begin nudging into the region from \nthe southwest by this evening. \n\nWinds aloft today will be stronger than they were on Sunday, \nwith the pressure gradient remaining fairly tight. Under similar\nmixing conditions, winds will likely be even breezier than they\nwere on Sunday, with gusts up to 30-35 mph possible. No wind \nheadlines should be needed, but the gusty winds will certainly \nbe noticeable. With much of the region near or past past peak \nfall foliage, these breezy conditions will likely defoliate much\nof the seasonal vegetation across the area. \n\nTemperatures will be near normal with highs in the lower 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal storm/nor'easter is forecast to \nimpact the region Thursday through Friday night. \n\nA vigorous upper-level trough is forecast to evolve across the \neastern U.S. later this week and into the weekend. This would \nsupport a surface low that may initially track near the Ohio \nValley, before a secondary low develops in the vicinity of the \nnorthern Mid- Atlantic. The model guidance, including ensemble \ndata, continues to be in good agreement that a deep upper-level \ntrough evolves in the East later this week. There are however \nsome differences with the handling of the embedded shortwaves \nand thus the details of the surface low. The details will be \nimportant because they will determine where the surface low \ntracks and where the main forcing for ascent becomes established\nalong with some colder air on the west/northwest side of the \ndeveloping coastal storm/nor'easter.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough would result in a\ncolder air mass moving in with time, however the initial air \nmass is not looking all that cold with the lack of a cold \nantecedent air mass. As a result, dynamic cooling would have to \ncome into play. This could allow for the air mass to become \nsufficiently cold enough for a rain/snow mix or even some snow \nacross our far western to northwestern zones (elevation \ndependent). There remains a variety of solutions across model \nguidance. It continues to appear at this time that the greatest \nprobabilities for some snow is across the I-80 corridor \n(Poconos). Otherwise, a decent rain event remains possible. The \namount of precipitation (rain and/or snow) will depend on the \nultimate development and track of the main surface low and the \nassociated thermal profiles that evolve across our region. The \nGEFS and ECMWF members suggest a spread still with the \nplacement/track of the surface low. The coastal impacts, \nincluding the potential for stronger winds and also tidal \nflooding, are less certain because if the surface low tracks \nright over our area that would lessen the residence time of the \n(stronger) onshore flow. The uncertainty remains with the \ndetails for Thursday through Friday night, therefore this time \nframe will continue to be monitored. It does look to turn colder\nFriday and over the weekend.",
